{
  "image_name": "1b7e8fd919b8b8b0c3ef55b6900998ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how AMPK helps maintain organelle quality and responds to cellular stress, linking mitochondria, DNA damage, and lysosomes. In mitochondria, reduced ATP production or oxidative stress raises AMP/ATP, activating AMPK and its mitochondrial pool, which promotes mitochondrial biogenesis, fission, and mitophagy to restore homeostasis.\n\nThe nuclear section shows AMPKα1 in DNA damage signaling. DNA lesions activate pathways involving ATM/ATR, CHK2, p53, and repair factors such as 53BP1 and EXO1. Calcium-dependent CaMKK2 also appears to activate AMPKα1. The diagram suggests AMPKα1 shuttles between nucleus and cytoplasm and influences both DNA repair and apoptotic outputs, including caspase 3 and pro-apoptotic gene expression.\n\nThe lysosomal part highlights a switch from intact to damaged lysosomes. Undamaged lysosomes are marked by LAMP1/2 and luminal glycans, whereas damage leads to ubiquitin accumulation and recruitment of factors including GAL9, USP9X, TAK1, and AMPK. Activated AMPK then seems to drive two coordinated responses: TFEB/TFE3-dependent lysosomal biogenesis and ULK1-linked lysophagy. The damaged lysosome is shown being wrapped by an LC3-positive autophagosome, illustrating selective removal of defective lysosomes.",
